  "title": "A cancel while an attempt is starting must stop it",
  "file": "src/scheduler.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "Something running inside the daemon reacts to the run's first 'running' update by cancelling the run straight away, before the scheduler has noted that the run is active. The run is marked cancelled, but the attempt is never stopped and goes on to deliver. No such in-process listener exists today: cancels from the HTTP API, MCP and Discord arrive in a later event-loop turn, after the scheduler has noted the run.",
   "reproduction": "unreproduced: reached only by an in-process listener that calls cancel synchronously on the store's 'running' update. I grepped every store listener (the scheduler, land queue, engine question wait, HTTP SSE, GitHub notifier and poller, Discord, feed) and none calls the scheduler's cancel. External cancels come in later event-loop tasks, after this.active.set.",
   "evidence": "At head, src/scheduler.ts:129-141: `const done = executeRun(...)...; this.active.set(run.id, { controller, done });`. executeRun runs synchronously up to its first await, and on the way executeScopedRun calls `deps.store.updateRun(runId, { status: \"running\", error: null, ... })` (engine.ts:221), whose publish() calls listeners synchronously (store.ts:708-719). If cancel() runs inside that window, `this.active.get(runId)` is undefined, so it takes the `finalizeRecordedCancellation` branch and never aborts the controller. Base had the same ordering: `executeRun(...)` before `this.active.set`, and an else-branch that wrote `status: \"cancelled\"` without aborting. So this is not new."
