  "title": "Startup recovery reconciles cancel-marked queued rows",
  "file": "src/scheduler.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "A user cancels a run that is waiting for GitHub, and the daemon crashes between recording the cancel and marking the run cancelled. After restart the run still shows as queued until its next GitHub retry time (up to about 5 minutes), and only then is it marked cancelled. Separately, a cancelled run can keep a stale wait record, which only matters for display, and only after a rollback to a release that doesn't know about waits.",
   "reproduction": "unreproduced: it needs a crash between two consecutive synchronous database writes in Scheduler.cancel. I traced the code instead.",
   "evidence": "Scheduler.cancel (scheduler.ts:147-158) first writes `updateRun(runId, { error: `cancelled by ${by}` })` and then, as a separate write, `finalizeRecordedCancellation`. start() (scheduler.ts:31) recovers only `status: [\"running\", \"waiting_input\"]`. nextQueuedRuns (store.ts:1808) filters with `coalesce(json_extract(runs.state_json, '$.githubWait.nextAttemptAt'), 0) <= ?`, so a cancel-marked queued row with a future nextAttemptAt isn't seen until it is due. Then tick's `if (finalizeRecordedCancellation(...)) continue;` finalizes it. So the cancel is delayed by at most one interval, not lost. At base the same crash would have lost the cancel entirely, because executeScopedRun resets `error: null`. Every path at head that sets a run to cancelled (finalizeRecordedCancellation and the engine's cancelled()) clears githubWait. So a cancelled row that still has githubWait can only come from an older release cancelling a parked run after a rollback, and the UI shows the wait line only for queued runs."
